<Base title="About" tab="about">
  <h1>About these compilations</h1>
  <div class="rules">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>A <strong>compiled transcription</strong> is one text in the original language, built from the transcriptions of the surviving manuscripts. It aims at the text most likely to match the original.</p>
    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Each compilation has one folder and one rule file, <code>RULES.md</code>. It lists the files to read, the rules, and the model that does the work.</li>
      <li>The model reads the rule file and runs one pass. The same inputs always give the same output, and the output is saved in the same folder, next to the rule file.</li>
      <li>A quick check, which takes about a second, confirms three things: every printed word is read by a manuscript, no word is doubled unless a manuscript doubles it, and the output matches its recorded hash.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>What is used, and what is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Used:</strong> transcriptions of ancient manuscripts in the original language, each one weighted by its <strong>proximity</strong>, meaning how close it was copied in time to the original.</li>
      <li><strong>Not used:</strong> printed or critical editions, recent reconstructions, translations, quotations, or images. Where no manuscript covers a line, the line is marked as not covered. It is never filled in from another source.</li>
      <li><strong>Images</strong> stay with the libraries that hold them. Each manuscript links to its holder's viewer where one exists.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Terms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>manuscript</strong> is any surviving handwritten source, whole or fragmentary.</li>
      <li>A <strong>transcription</strong> is the characters of one manuscript in typed form.</li>
      <li><strong>Alignment</strong> means lining up each word of each transcription with the same word in the others.</li>
      <li>A <strong>variant</strong> is a place where the transcriptions read differently. A <strong>place</strong> is one stretch of the text where they disagree.</li>
      <li>A <strong>singular reading</strong> is found in one manuscript alone. It has no vote.</li>
      <li>An <strong>eye-skip</strong> happens when a copyist's eye jumps from one word to a similar word further on and leaves out the words between. It counts as a copying slip.</li>
      <li>A <strong>line</strong> is the numbered unit used for reference, written as chapter:line.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
